    "evidence_text": "Offensive language: The GermEval  shared task focused on Offensive language identification in German tweets.\n\nNot Offensive (NOT): Posts that do not contain offense or profanity; Offensive (OFF): We label a post as offensive if it contains any form of non-acceptable language (profanity) or a targeted offense, which can be veiled or direct.\n\nLevel B: Categorization of Offensive Language\tLevel B categorizes the\n\nLevel C: Offensive Language Target Identification\tLevel C categorizes the targets of insults and threats as individual (IND), group (GRP), and other (OTH).\n\nAlthough `he is' is lower in offensive content we kept it as a keyword to avoid gender bias.\n\nOffensive Language Detection\tThe performance on discriminating between offensive (OFF) and non-offensive (NOT) posts is reported in Table TABREF18 .\n\nOffensive Language Target Identification\tThe results of the offensive target identification experiment are reported in Table TABREF20 .\n\nConclusion and Future Work\tThis paper presents OLID, a new dataset with annotation of type and target of offensive language.\n\nOLID is the official dataset of the shared task SemEval 2019 Task 6: Identifying and Categorizing Offensive Language in Social Media (OffensEval)  .",
